 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <p>One or more questions for each outcome at the top of the page; Figure 1 covers outcome 1, Figure 2 outcome 2, the call quiz outcome 3 and Figure 3 outcome 5.</p>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> Lead takes the flight through controlled airspace, then into a valley where you both want lookout and room to manoeuvre. Which formations?</summary><p>Cruise through controlled airspace; combat cruise for the valley, which gives flexibility and terrain masking. Combat spread suits open terrain and maximum lookout.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> In combat cruise you have drifted to lead’s 6 o’clock. Why move?</summary><p>The source says to avoid long periods within 30° of trail (5 to 7 o’clock): that area is for transit only. Return to the preferred 30–45° bearings.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> In parade the antenna has moved forward of the aileron cutout and the stack is buried below the wing. What do you fix first?</summary><p>Altitude first: you are high (stack not visible), so step down until the stack sits on the wing’s leading edge. Then fix the bearing: you are off the bearing line, acute or sucked depending on which way the antenna has moved.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> Why can’t you fly good parade from further out?</summary><p>At a distance you cannot pick up the lead’s small changes, so your corrections become large and late. Small distances mean small inputs.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> Lead says “Angry, push UHF TAC.” What do you do?</summary><p>Change to the UHF tactical frequency. When lead says “Angry” there, answer “Angry 1-2.”</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> Where may the flight do its check-ins?</summary><p>Only on its tactical frequency, never on ATC, area common or CTAF frequencies.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> During the rendezvous you are closing fast. Should you pull the power back?</summary><p>Not as the first tool: the rendezvous is flown by radius of turn, with power left alone unless the instructor directs otherwise. If you pass under and ahead of lead, it is an underrun: step down, keep sight, level the wings, idle, call “underrunning”, then join outside.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> When does wing start the breakup turn?</summary><p>As lead approaches the 3/9 line abeam the leading edge of the wing; the three-second count is only a technique.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> In cloud you lose sight of lead in a turn toward you. What do you do and say?</summary><p>Go to instruments, call “lost sight” with your altitude, and take off about 10% power, all at once. In a turn into you, add a command such as “lead roll out” to make separation quickly.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> In a night formation, the lead’s strobes are reflecting off the cloud you are flying in. What does the US rule allow?</summary><p>The pilot may turn the strobes off in flight when they reflect off cloud, and turn the anti-collision lights off when their output is a hazard.</p></details>

